WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.549 --> 00:00:03.919
If you've seen "The Batman," did you wonder
where else you'd seen the actor who played

00:00:03.919 --> 00:00:05.270
Carmine Falcone?

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:10.200
Keep watching for some of the many recognizable
roles John Turturro has played.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.660
Turturro caught director Spike Lee's eye with
his performance in 1987's "Five Corners,"

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.529
so Lee cast him in his 1989 film "Do the Right
Thing."

00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:22.759
In an interview with Vanity Fair, Turturro
said Lee sent him the script and asked which

00:00:22.759 --> 00:00:24.490
part he wanted to play.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:25.490
Turturro answered:

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:28.619
"I said I'd like to play the racist guy because
I thought that's what it was about."

00:00:28.619 --> 00:00:32.340
Set in a predominantly Black neighborhood
in Brooklyn, "Do the Right Thing" explores

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:37.239
racial tensions between an Italian-American
family running a pizzeria and their neighbors.

00:00:37.239 --> 00:00:42.530
Lee himself plays pizza delivery guy Mookie
and Turturro plays Pino, the racist son of

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:43.840
the pizzeria's owner.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:47.679
In their key scene together, Pino names his
favorite celebrities — who are all Black

00:00:47.679 --> 00:00:48.760
— but then says…

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.160
"They're not Black.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.950
I mean, let me explain myself.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.260
They're not really Black.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.449
They're Black, but they're not really Black."

00:00:57.449 --> 00:01:01.449
Lee broke down the scene to Entertainment
Weekly, pointing out that line as key:

00:01:01.449 --> 00:01:04.050
"Pino, when he's talking, it's not based upon
fiction.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:08.070
... I've heard this argument where the most
racist people, their favorite athlete, entertainer,

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:09.980
or comedian, is Black."

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:13.820
Lee admitted he was intimidated doing that
scene, calling Turturro "a beast."

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.210
"Do the Right Thing" kicked off a long collaboration,
with Turturro appearing in several more of

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:23.380
Spike Lee's movies, including "Jungle Fever,"
"Clockers," and "Mo' Better Blues."

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:27.490
John Turturro is only in "The Big Lebowski"
for a few minutes, but he made a big impression

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:29.600
as bowling competitor The Jesus.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.030
Dressed in a purple jumpsuit, Jesus Quintana
does an elaborate ritual before he throws

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:35.280
his ball down the lane.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.810
After getting a strike, he does a dance, insults
Jeff Bridges' The Dude, and leaves.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.680
It's a fever dream in slow motion.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.120
The eccentric character was a collaboration
between Turturro and Joel and Ethan Coen,

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.990
the brothers who wrote, directed, and produced
the movie.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:53.160
In an interview with Vanity Fair, Turturro
said The Jesus is inspired by a character

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:57.650
he portrayed in a play a decade earlier: He
asked for the painted pinky nail and brought

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:58.790
the hair net.

00:01:58.790 --> 00:01:59.790
Turturro said,

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:01.930
"When you only have five minutes, you've gotta
put everything in.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:05.390
I just kept trying to come up with things
to make [the Coen brothers] laugh."

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:07.090
Turturro said the scene shocked even him.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:08.090
He said,

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.590
"I didn't think they were going to use all
of that and then when they showed me the cut

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.060
of it, I was embarrassed.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.700
I was laughing, but I was embarrassed.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.980
I was like, 'Oh god, I can't believe I did
all those things.'"

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.980
That embarrassment didn't hold him back from
bringing Jesus back for a sequel movie, "The

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:24.220
Jesus Rolls," which he wrote, directed, and
starred in.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:25.220
Turturro explained,

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:29.120
"Everything he does is a masterpiece in his
own mind, despite the fact that everything

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.190
he does backfires."

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:35.840
Continuing his work with the Coen brothers,
John Turturro starred in 2000's "O Brother,

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:36.840
Where Art Thou?"

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.090
alongside George Clooney and Tim Blake Nelson.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:43.390
The three of them play escaped convicts in
Depression-era Mississippi who embark on an

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:45.350
episodic journey to get back home.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:49.850
The film is loosely based on Homer's "The
Odyssey," with Clooney's Ulysses Everett McGill

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.640
taking the role of Odysseus.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:57.010
Turturro's Pete and Nelson's Delmar are dimwitted,
compared to Ulysses' cunning, but they follow

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.920
him in hopes of finding hidden treasure.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.180
"O Brother, Where Art Thou?" is particularly
known for its iconic soundtrack of old folk

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:07.720
songs, which become part of the story as the
group finds radio fame as The Soggybottom

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:08.720
Boys.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.069
Turturro said of working with the Coens,

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:13.910
"It's always a new world that they create
and it's fun to fill that imaginary world

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:14.910
out.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:15.910
It's never the same."

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:19.520
He also appeared in their movie "Miller's
Crossing" as the bookie Bernie Bernbaum and

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.850
as the title playwright in the Coens' film
"Barton Fink."

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:23.790
"Look in your heart!

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.010
I'm praying to you!

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:30.230
Look in your heart!

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:33.459
I'm praying to you!"

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:37.720
John Turturro hasn't done a lot of TV until
recently, which is why his guest appearance

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.330
on "Monk" was both special and unusual.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:44.580
Mr. Monk himself, Tony Shalhoub, asked Turturro
to come on the show, since the two of them

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:48.780
are friends and had worked together before
on "Barton Fink" and the play "Waiting for

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:49.780
Godot."

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:53.900
Turturro comes into the story in the Season
2 episode "Mr. Monk and the Three Pies," playing

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.010
Adrian Monk's brother, Ambrose.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:04:00.239
Like Adrian, who's known for his obsessive
compulsive disorder and long list of phobias,

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:03.180
Ambrose Monk has his own collection of fears
and quirks.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:08.120
He hasn't left their childhood house in over
30 years because of his severe agoraphobia.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.430
That's not the reason the two of them are
estranged, though.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:15.010
They haven't talked in years because Ambrose
blames himself for the death of Adrian's wife.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:18.849
But when Ambrose thinks his neighbor killed
someone, he calls Adrian to solve it, and

00:04:18.849 --> 00:04:20.650
the two awkwardly reunite.

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:24.010
It's a favorite episode of Shalhoub's, as
the strained relationship between the two

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:26.699
brothers reveals another side to his character.

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:28.249
Shalhoub told USA Today:

00:04:28.249 --> 00:04:30.629
"He's more abrupt and less sympathetic.

00:04:30.629 --> 00:04:33.150
It's a healthy thing to see that he's not
a complete saint."

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:37.680
Turturro won an Emmy for Outstanding Guest
Actor in a Comedy Series and ended up coming

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.190
back for two more episodes: Season 4's "Mr.
Monk Goes Home Again" and Season 7's "Mr.

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:44.669
Monk's 100th Case."

00:04:44.669 --> 00:04:48.659
Before Turturro's second episode aired, Shalhoub
praised him to The New York Times:

00:04:48.659 --> 00:04:52.900
"Somehow in two episodes he's managed to take
the character and make him a fully rounded,

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:53.970
complete part of the show."

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:59.610
In four "Transformers" movies, Turturro plays
the antagonist-turned-ally Agent Seymour Simmons

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:01.539
of the government's secretive Sector Seven.

00:05:01.539 --> 00:05:06.300
At the Montclair Film Festival, Turturro said
he based his performance on the movie's director,

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:10.139
Michael Bay, as he's done with other directors
and executive producers.

00:05:10.139 --> 00:05:14.319
This role was a big departure from Turturro's
usual independent movies, but he took the

00:05:14.319 --> 00:05:15.710
job because of his son.

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:16.710
He told The Independent,

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.900
"I never did any of those kind of movies 'till
my kids asked me to, then they kept asking

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:21.900
me to do it.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.029
I didn't do it for a paycheck.

00:05:23.029 --> 00:05:25.550
I had fun, tried to get into the spirit of
it.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:27.999
It's more of a sketch of a person than an
oil painting."

00:05:27.999 --> 00:05:32.099
He says he prefers "medium-sized films" to
the big budget ones, although he said those

00:05:32.099 --> 00:05:33.850
roles have become harder to find.

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:34.850
He told Variety,

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:36.719
"Now there are no medium-sized films.

00:05:36.719 --> 00:05:38.830
There are small films or giant films.

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:40.189
Thank god for television."

00:05:40.189 --> 00:05:45.300
Now, Turturro is back with one of the biggest
films in recent memory, "The Batman."

